Comment:
BATCH inference child (README.md §M1). Submits one Bedrock batch job for all N records, parks on a task token until the job finishes, collects the output, and summarizes. No lease on the SUBMISSION path: the in-progress-job limit is account-level and service-enforced, so a local cap would serialize submissions Bedrock is willing to accept in parallel (§1.1). Standalone and independently startable given a plan whose $.data.batch.can_batch is true. Input: {"uuid":"<correlation>","input":{...},"data":{<plan>}}

FOUR states, and the repair tail is a NESTED CALL rather than a copied spine (README.md §P8). Repairing failed records IS sync inference — lease, chain walk, checkpoint, release — so this machine invokes the sync CHILD MACHINE for it instead of duplicating four of its states here. What that buys: run_inference and the sync ASL are untouched, there is exactly one lease/checkpoint/release spine in the stack, and 'either child is startable on its own given a plan' still holds because the repair lives here and not in the orchestrator. What it costs: the start graph gains an edge, so it is stated as an invariant — orchestrator → {sync, batch}, batch → sync, sync → nothing, ACYCLIC, and a test pins it. A machine that could reach itself is a recursion with a real Bedrock bill.

The route into the repair is a Catch on 'Collect batch output', not a Choice. collect_batch RAISES BatchRecordsIncompleteException over already-committed S3 state, and the exception is control flow rather than a fault: the caught path summarizes normally and this execution SUCCEEDS. The cost is deliberate and accepted — a partial batch renders as a red state in the console and emits a LambdaFunctionsFailed datapoint — because a run that had a problem SHOULD be legible in the execution graph months later, where a boolean on a payload has to be dug out of the history. It is also the only mechanism that can discriminate: a .sync:2 child failure reaches its parent as States.TaskFailed, never as the child's own error name, so an equivalent Catch one level up in the orchestrator could not tell a repairable batch from any other failure.
